sxml xpath - Evaluate an XPath 4.0 expression

Usage: sxml xpath [--context-item=FILE] [--param=NAME=VALUE]... [--method=METHOD] [--allow-file-access] [--collection=DIR] [--code-language=LIBRARY]... [--output=FILE] [--force] EXPRESSION

Evaluates EXPRESSION as XPath 4.0 and writes the result to standard
output, by the adaptive method with one item per line unless --method
names another.

--context-item=FILE parses FILE and makes it the context item; without
it there is no context item. --param=NAME=VALUE binds $NAME to
xs:untypedAtomic(VALUE). fn:doc reads local files. --allow-file-access
lets the expression read and write files with the EXPath File
functions (namespace http://expath.org/ns/file); without it they do
not exist. An EXPRESSION that starts with - must follow --.

--output=FILE (-o FILE) writes the result to FILE instead, and only
when the command succeeds; a FILE that exists is replaced only with
--force.

An error is reported on standard error as
  expression:LINE:COLUMN: err:CODE: description

Exit status: 0 on success, 1 for an error in the expression or a
context document that is not well-formed, 3 when a file cannot be
read or written.

Options:
  -h, --help                   Show this help and exit
      --context-item=FILE      XML document to use as the context item; - reads standard input
      --param=NAME=VALUE       Bind $NAME to xs:untypedAtomic(VALUE) (repeatable)
      --method=METHOD          Serialization method of the result (one of: adaptive, xml, xhtml, html, text, json, csv; default: adaptive)
      --allow-file-access      Let the query read and write files (EXPath File functions)
      --collection=DIR         Directory whose XML files are the default collection
      --code-language=LIBRARY  Load a tree-sitter language for tree-sitter:parse from a shared library (repeatable)
  -o, --output=FILE            Write the result to FILE instead of standard output
      --force                  Replace files that exist instead of refusing

Arguments:
  EXPRESSION  XPath 4.0 expression to evaluate

Examples:
  sxml xpath '1 to 3'
  sxml xpath --context-item=draft.xml '//t[bcp14 = "MUST"]/@anchor/string()'
  sxml xpath --param=n=5 'xs:integer($n) * 2'
  sxml xpath --method=json 'map { "a": [1, 2] }'
  sxml xpath --context-item=draft.xml --output=anchors.txt '//t/@anchor/string()'
